WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.757
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level four.

2
00:00:06.057 --> 00:00:09.466
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.766 --> 00:00:17.016
Unit fourteen: Ocean & Maritime Law. Today you will read and write binding duties with shall, shall not and may.

4
00:00:17.316 --> 00:00:19.748
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.026
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.883 --> 00:00:26.349
Territorial waters.

7
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:31.544
Territorial waters means the sea close to a coast that belongs to that country.

8
00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:35.636
Foreign ships need permission to fish in territorial waters.

9
00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:39.831
Your turn. Say: Territorial waters.

10
00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:44.806
Territorial waters.

11
00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:47.422
Exclusive economic zone.

12
00:00:48.417 --> 00:00:53.713
Exclusive economic zone means the sea area where a coastal state controls fishing and energy.

13
00:00:54.317 --> 00:00:58.093
The exclusive economic zone extends two hundred nautical miles.

14
00:00:58.683 --> 00:01:02.462
Your turn. Say: Exclusive economic zone.

15
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.218
Exclusive economic zone.

16
00:01:07.983 --> 00:01:09.449
Maritime boundary.

17
00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:15.166
Maritime boundary means the line that separates the sea areas of two countries.

18
00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:18.678
The maritime boundary was drawn after long talks.

19
00:01:19.283 --> 00:01:22.481
Your turn. Say: Maritime boundary.

20
00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:26.810
Maritime boundary.

21
00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:29.027
Freedom of navigation.

22
00:01:30.017 --> 00:01:34.478
Freedom of navigation means the right of ships to sail through international waters.

23
00:01:35.083 --> 00:01:38.476
Freedom of navigation keeps global trade moving.

24
00:01:39.083 --> 00:01:42.467
Your turn. Say: Freedom of navigation.

25
00:01:45.333 --> 00:01:47.043
Freedom of navigation.

26
00:01:47.783 --> 00:01:49.469
Deep sea resources.

27
00:01:50.483 --> 00:01:55.641
Deep sea resources means minerals, oil and life found far below the ocean surface.

28
00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:59.260
Deep sea resources may power future batteries.

29
00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:03.211
Your turn. Say: Deep sea resources.

30
00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:07.637
Deep sea resources.

31
00:02:08.383 --> 00:02:09.640
Fishing rights.

32
00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:14.646
Fishing rights means the legal permission to catch fish in a particular area.

33
00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:18.144
Fishing rights caused arguments between neighbours.

34
00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.669
Your turn. Say: Fishing rights.

35
00:02:24.533 --> 00:02:25.825
Fishing rights.

36
00:02:26.583 --> 00:02:27.724
Law of the sea.

37
00:02:28.717 --> 00:02:32.876
Law of the sea means the international rules that govern the use of the oceans.

38
00:02:33.483 --> 00:02:36.296
The law of the sea divides the ocean into zones.

39
00:02:36.883 --> 00:02:40.337
Your turn. Say: Law of the sea.

40
00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:44.782
Law of the sea.

41
00:02:45.517 --> 00:02:46.971
Maritime dispute.

42
00:02:47.983 --> 00:02:52.746
Maritime dispute means a disagreement between countries about sea areas or rights.

43
00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:56.801
A maritime dispute was sent to an international tribunal.

44
00:02:57.417 --> 00:03:00.684
Your turn. Say: Maritime dispute.

45
00:03:03.533 --> 00:03:04.941
Maritime dispute.

46
00:03:05.733 --> 00:03:09.640
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:10.483 --> 00:03:16.352
Visal, a treaty says 'Every state shall respect the freedom of navigation.' Why 'shall' and not 'must'?

48
00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:30.429
In treaties and laws, 'shall' creates a binding duty. It sounds formal and is common with third person subjects. 'May' gives permission, and 'shall not' forbids: 'No state shall claim the high seas.'

49
00:03:31.517 --> 00:03:33.563
Is 'shall' used in everyday speech?

50
00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:44.979
Not for obligation. Today we use it mostly for offers and suggestions, like 'Shall I help?'. In legal English it keeps its strong sense of duty.

51
00:03:46.083 --> 00:03:47.886
How do I read a legal sentence?

52
00:03:48.983 --> 00:04:00.265
Find the subject, the modal and the action. 'Coastal states may use resources in their zone' means permission; 'shall protect' means obligation. Be careful with shall not.

53
00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:02.784
Part three: grammar.

54
00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:07.763
Grammar focus: Legal modals: shall and shall not and may.

55
00:04:08.063 --> 00:04:09.331
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:10.017 --> 00:04:13.026
Every state shall respect the freedom of navigation.

57
00:04:13.917 --> 00:04:16.845
No state shall claim sovereignty over the high seas.

58
00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:21.723
Coastal states may manage fishing rights in their exclusive economic zone.

59
00:04:22.667 --> 00:04:32.346
In laws and treaties, shall = a binding duty, shall not = a ban, may = permission. In everyday English, shall is mainly for offers (Shall I help?).

60
00:04:33.083 --> 00:04:36.978
Be careful! Don't say: No state shall not block the sea.

61
00:04:38.933 --> 00:04:41.397
Say: No state shall block the sea.

62
00:04:41.697 --> 00:04:43.302
Do not use a double negative.

63
00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.718
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:47.533 --> 00:04:48.848
Who Owns the Ocean?.

65
00:04:49.148 --> 00:04:53.086
The law of the sea divides the oceans into zones with different rights.

66
00:04:54.100 --> 00:05:01.800
A coastal state has full control of its territorial waters, and it may use its exclusive economic zone for fishing and energy.

67
00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:09.699
All ships shall enjoy freedom of navigation beyond territorial waters, and no state shall block international shipping routes.

68
00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:17.506
A maritime boundary is often unclear, so a maritime dispute may arise over fishing rights or deep sea resources.

69
00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:24.122
Parties shall settle such disputes peacefully, usually through negotiation or an international tribunal.

70
00:05:25.233 --> 00:05:29.165
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:30.017 --> 00:05:32.713
In a treaty, 'shall' usually creates a blank.

72
00:05:33.013 --> 00:05:36.190
A, duty. B, joke. C, question.

73
00:05:39.733 --> 00:05:41.536
The answer is A: duty.

74
00:05:42.683 --> 00:05:44.590
Which sentence shows permission?

75
00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:50.726
A, States shall pay a fee.. B, States may fish in their zone.. C, States shall not enter..

76
00:05:54.267 --> 00:05:56.986
The answer is B: States may fish in their zone..

77
00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:02.054
The right of ships to sail freely at sea is called blank of navigation.

78
00:06:02.354 --> 00:06:05.728
A, debris. B, freedom. C, closure.

79
00:06:09.267 --> 00:06:10.860
The answer is B: freedom.

80
00:06:12.017 --> 00:06:15.700
A disagreement over where a sea border lies is a maritime blank.

81
00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:19.618
A, dispute. B, summit. C, alliance.

82
00:06:23.167 --> 00:06:24.864
The answer is A: dispute.

83
00:06:26.017 --> 00:06:29.154
According to the reading, parties shall settle disputes…

84
00:06:29.454 --> 00:06:33.432
A, by force. B, peacefully. C, secretly.

85
00:06:36.967 --> 00:06:39.256
The answer is B: peacefully.

86
00:06:40.467 --> 00:06:43.398
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:47.898
Why might countries disagree over rights to deep sea resources?

88
00:06:56.683 --> 00:06:58.056
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:58.356 --> 00:07:26.634
Countries disagree over deep sea resources because the minerals and fish lie far from any coast, so no state clearly owns them. Rich nations have the technology to extract them first, while poorer countries argue that the seabed belongs to all humanity. Different readings of the law of the sea, together with unclear maritime boundaries, can turn these arguments into serious disputes.

90
00:07:26.934 --> 00:07:28.748
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:35.228
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and legal modals: shall and shall not and may.

92
00:07:35.528 --> 00:07:41.629
Today's value is global legal awareness. Respect that other societies organise justice differently.

93
00:07:41.929 --> 00:07:44.500
See you in Unit fifteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:48.299
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

